{"prdMarkdown":"# PRD: In-Memory Repo Read Caching\n\n## Problem\nEvery execution of the analysis pipeline walks the entire target repository filesystem from scratch in `src/pipeline/repoReader.js`. For repeated runs against the same repository path, this repeated file traversal and content extraction introduces unnecessary disk I/O latency, slowing down pipeline feedback cycles.\n\n## Goals\n- Cache repository file reads in memory across pipeline invocations targeting the same resolved repo path.\n- Enable instant re-runs on previously scanned paths without redundant filesystem walks.\n- Provide an explicit force-refresh mechanism (via query param or request payload) allowing users to bypass the cache and trigger a full re-scan when desired.\n\n## Non-goals\n- Persistent cache storage in PostgreSQL (`src/db/`) — cache will remain in-memory for this iteration.\n- Automated background filesystem watchers (e.g., `fs.watch` / file system event listening).\n- Dynamic remote repository clone caching or cache keying based on remote git commit SHAs unless paths match.\n\n## User Impact\n- Developers and API consumers running multiple analyses against local projects experience significantly faster turnaround times on repeated queries.\n- Users updating local code between runs can provide a force-refresh flag to ensure latest disk changes are captured.\n\n## Open Product Questions\n- `[PRODUCT_DECISION]` Where and how should the force-refresh option be exposed in the API contract and user interface (e.g., `refresh=true` query parameter vs. `forceRefresh` JSON body field)?\n- `[ASSUMPTION_MADE]` Who is affected: Assumed this primarily targets local path scans, as behavior for ephemeral or cloned temporary directories was skipped during requirements gathering.","trdMarkdown":"# TRD: In-Memory Repo Read Caching\n\n## Architecture Notes\n- **In-Memory Store in `src/pipeline/repoReader.js`**: Introduce an internal cache store (e.g., a `Map` or LRU structure) within `src/pipeline/repoReader.js` that maps normalized repository paths to parsed repository artifacts (file lists, manifests, README contents).\n- **Path Normalization**: Cache keys must use normalized paths via `path.resolve` to avoid misses or redundant entries due to relative paths, trailing slashes, or varying path representations.\n- **Defensive Copying**: Downstream pipeline steps (`src/pipeline/index.js`, `src/pipeline/clarify.js`, `src/pipeline/blastRadius.js`) must receive cloned or immutable copies of cached data to prevent accidental mutation of the in-memory cache entries.\n- **Route & Pipeline Plumbing**: Update `src/routes/sessions.js` to parse and validate the force-refresh parameter (e.g., `forceRefresh` or `?refresh=true`) and pass it through the pipeline orchestrator in `src/pipeline/index.js` down to `repoReader.js`.\n\n## Edge Cases & Dependencies\n- **Path Format Discrepancies**: Relative paths vs. absolute paths or symlinks targeting the same workspace. Keying must be normalized using `path.resolve` in `src/pipeline/repoReader.js`.\n- **Stale Working Trees**: Modifying local files or switching git branches between runs will return stale cache entries if force-refresh is omitted.\n- **Memory Consumption (OOM)**: An unbounded in-memory cache storing file contents across multiple large repositories introduces Node.js process Out-Of-Memory (OOM) risks.\n- **Object Mutation**: Downstream stages mutating file lists in-place will corrupt subsequent cached runs unless objects are deep-copied or frozen.\n- **Temp Directory Invalidation**: Ephemeral git clone directories created with random path prefixes will result in a 0% cache hit rate.\n\n## Engineering Questions\n- `[ENGINEERING_DECISION]` What eviction or TTL strategy (e.g., LRU cache, max repo limit, or time-based expiry) should be implemented to prevent process out-of-memory crashes with the in-memory cache?\n- `[ENGINEERING_DECISION]` Should the cache store full file contents or only directory file listings, considering memory footprint versus disk I/O savings?\n- `[ENGINEERING_DECISION]` Should cache entries detect workspace changes automatically (e.g., via HEAD commit SHA or file mtime checks), or rely strictly on the manual force-refresh trigger?\n\n## Estimate\n- **Estimated Effort**: 2-3 days (up to 7 days worst case if eviction management and automatic SHA/mtime change detection are required)."}